.TH TAIL 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
tail \- copy the last lines (or bytes) of files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tail
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR number \ |\ \-n
.IR number ]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tail
copies each
.I file
to standard output, beginning at a designated place. By default the
last 10 lines of each file are copied.
.PP
When more than one
.I file
operand is given, each file's output is preceded by a banner line of
the form
.RS
.nf
==> \fIfile\fR <==
.fi
.RE
with a blank line before every banner after the first. No banner is
printed when there is exactly one operand (or none).
.PP
Each file is read into memory in full before any output is produced,
so that "relative to the end of the file" can be answered even when
the input is not seekable (a pipe or a FIFO). This means memory use
is proportional to the size of the input, not to
.IR number .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " number"
Copy bytes rather than lines, starting at the place
.I number
designates (see OPERANDS below for the
.RI [ + | \- ] number
grammar). Mutually paired with
.B \-n
in the sense that whichever of
.B \-c
or
.B \-n
appears last on the command line wins; giving both is not a usage
error.
.TP
.BI \-f " "
Do not exit after copying the last lines of a file; instead, wait for
data to be appended and copy it as it arrives ("follow"). If neither
.B \-c
nor
.B \-n
is given together with
.BR \-f ,
the default of the last 10 lines still applies before following
begins.
.RS
.PP
For a single operand that is not a regular file (a pipe, a FIFO, or
standard input fed from one), following is a plain blocking read
loop: it ends the moment the writer closes its end, since a closed
pipe can never grow again.
.PP
For one or more regular\-file operands, following instead polls each
file's size roughly five times a second and copies whatever has been
appended since the last poll, round\-robining across every followed
file in argument order (printing a fresh banner whenever the file
being drained changes, if there is more than one). A
.B \-f
run has no other exit condition for a regular file \(em it keeps
polling forever, since a still file can always grow again later. The
process still terminates the normal way on an interrupt signal.
.PP
If a non\-regular operand (a pipe or FIFO) is mixed in among several
file operands under
.BR \-f ,
that one operand only ever receives its initial, one\-time tail: it
is not polled or followed, since it has no size for
.B tail
to poll.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-n " number"
Copy lines rather than bytes, starting at the place
.I number
designates. This is the default mode when neither
.B \-c
nor
.B \-n
is given, with a default
.I number
of 10 (equivalent to
.BR "\-n \-10" ).
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file. If
.B \-
is given, standard input is used at that point in the sequence. If no
.I file
operand is given at all, standard input is used.
.PP
The
.I number
argument to
.B \-c
or
.B \-n
is a decimal integer, optionally signed:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
A leading
.B +
counts from the beginning of the file, 1\-based:
.B "\-n +1"
starts at the first line,
.B "\-c +1"
at the first byte.
.I number
must be non\-zero;
.B +0
is a usage error.
.IP \(bu 2
A leading
.B \-
or no sign at all counts backward from the end of the file: the
.I number
trailing lines or bytes are copied. A
.I number
of 0 copies nothing.
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All input was copied successfully (and, with
.BR \-f ,
following ended cleanly \(em only possible for a single non\-regular
operand whose writer closed).
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (a bad
.BR \-c / \-n
argument, or a file that could not be opened, read, or written);
processing continues with the next operand.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR head (1),
.BR cat (1),
.BR wc (1)
